WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.125 --> 00:00:03.336
This is a chart of the gender pay gap 
in developed economies.

00:00:03.837 --> 00:00:07.257
In the US, women are paid 
17% less than men.

00:00:07.632 --> 00:00:10.135
And in Japan, 22%.

00:00:10.552 --> 00:00:12.637
On the other end, 
there's Iceland...

00:00:12.721 --> 00:00:15.306
where the pay gap is around 10%.

00:00:15.557 --> 00:00:17.767
It's one of the few countries in the world

00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:20.353
where women are paid 
almost as much as men.

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:23.231
But that wasn't the case 
about 50 years ago.

00:00:23.565 --> 00:00:27.068
In 1975, the gender pay gap 
in the US

00:00:27.068 --> 00:00:28.903
was 37%.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:31.281
Japan was 42%...

00:00:31.406 --> 00:00:34.117
and Iceland was at 40%.

00:00:34.576 --> 00:00:37.996
So how did it go from here... to here?

00:00:38.997 --> 00:00:41.166
To figure that out, you have to 
take a look

00:00:41.166 --> 00:00:44.669
at what happened 
on this day in 1975.

00:00:46.921 --> 00:00:49.466
This idea was to show...

00:00:49.591 --> 00:00:53.136
that we could bring
society to a standstill.

00:00:53.136 --> 00:00:55.638
If we were not there,
nothing was done.

00:00:56.056 --> 00:01:01.603
We expected a lot of women to
show up at the demonstration,

00:01:01.603 --> 00:01:04.814
But not in our wildest 
dreams did we expect this.

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:07.859
This is the story of how 
Icelandic women

00:01:07.859 --> 00:01:11.154
shut down their country 
and changed it forever.

00:01:19.037 --> 00:01:21.081
Iceland gained independence 
from Denmark

00:01:21.081 --> 00:01:24.709
and became a parliamentary democracy 
in 1944.

00:01:24.751 --> 00:01:26.586
“Iceland separated from Denmark 

00:01:26.586 --> 00:01:28.505
and regained full self-government.”

00:01:28.546 --> 00:01:30.465
The conservative Independence Party

00:01:30.465 --> 00:01:33.510
became the strongest political force 
in the country

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:37.639
and held the position of Prime Minister 
for almost 20 years.

00:01:37.931 --> 00:01:39.891
Under this party's leadership

00:01:39.891 --> 00:01:42.894
policies that enforced gender inequity 
were widespread...

00:01:43.186 --> 00:01:45.188
especially in the workplace.

00:01:45.814 --> 00:01:48.399
By the 70s, women made up 
the majority

00:01:48.399 --> 00:01:51.111
of poorly paid, undervalued jobs...

00:01:51.736 --> 00:01:54.739
and were forced out of the labor market 
to work at home.

00:01:55.115 --> 00:01:58.076
In response to decades of conservative policies

00:01:58.076 --> 00:02:00.537
a new women's group called 
The Redstockings

00:02:00.537 --> 00:02:03.373
took radical steps to demand equal rights.

00:02:03.748 --> 00:02:07.085
Like when they crowned a cow 
at the Young Miss Iceland pageant

00:02:07.085 --> 00:02:09.587
to bring attention to unfair 
beauty standards.

00:02:11.005 --> 00:02:13.550
And crucified a doll 
dressed like a housewife

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:15.760
to protest women's domestic labor.

00:02:16.052 --> 00:02:19.097
It is a very active group.

00:02:19.097 --> 00:02:20.598
A bit anarchistic.

00:02:20.765 --> 00:02:22.976
This is Elisabet Gunnarsdottir

00:02:22.976 --> 00:02:25.353
a founding member of the Redstockings.

00:02:25.645 --> 00:02:27.605
We felt that there
were so many

00:02:27.605 --> 00:02:31.276
aspects of our lives that 
hadn't been discussed,

00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:35.446
that had stayed the same 
for such a long time.

00:02:35.446 --> 00:02:37.365
Workplace inequity was a big issue

00:02:37.365 --> 00:02:40.201
Elisabet and the Redstockings 
wanted to challenge

00:02:40.201 --> 00:02:43.079
and they took to the streets 
to draw attention to it.

00:02:43.246 --> 00:02:45.748
In the first of May 
demonstration in 1970,

00:02:45.748 --> 00:02:49.085
We carried a big statue 
on our shoulders.

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:53.173
And we got it from a play
that had been put up

00:02:53.173 --> 00:02:56.926
in Reykjavík that winter,
about women going on strike.

00:02:57.343 --> 00:03:00.638
But to make an impact, they wanted 
a full blown strike.

00:03:00.805 --> 00:03:03.057
And that was going to be complicated...

00:03:03.266 --> 00:03:06.477
because organizing a strike 
outside of trade unions

00:03:06.477 --> 00:03:09.939
and employers associations 
was illegal in Iceland.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:13.318
So the Redstockings who weren't 
part of either group

00:03:13.318 --> 00:03:15.361
couldn't act on their idea.

00:03:15.987 --> 00:03:18.281
Until 1975

00:03:18.281 --> 00:03:20.325
when the United Nations kicked off...

00:03:20.325 --> 00:03:22.243
“International Women's Year.”

00:03:22.493 --> 00:03:26.998
“I would like to welcome you all 
to this really historic event.”

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:29.209
This was the first global conference

00:03:29.209 --> 00:03:30.835
dedicated to women's issues.

00:03:31.085 --> 00:03:33.588
“Women form part of

00:03:33.588 --> 00:03:36.132
virtually every delegation.”

00:03:38.009 --> 00:03:39.719
And governments around the world

00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:42.764
planned events to address 
gender discrimination.

00:03:43.139 --> 00:03:45.850
In Iceland, the Prime Minister's 
office selected

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:48.519
representatives from different 
women's associations

00:03:48.519 --> 00:03:50.980
across the country to make preparations.

00:03:51.314 --> 00:03:53.399
The committee was politically diverse.

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:55.818
It included teachers, single mothers

00:03:55.818 --> 00:03:58.655
conservatives, and liberals like 
the Redstockings...

00:03:58.905 --> 00:04:00.448
to make sure the event reflected

00:04:00.448 --> 00:04:03.034
the views of women across Iceland.

00:04:03.409 --> 00:04:05.745
The Redstockings took this opportunity

00:04:05.745 --> 00:04:09.040
to propose that a women strike 
against the gender pay gap

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:11.960
was the best way to mark 
International Women's Year.

00:04:12.293 --> 00:04:14.254
The conservative women’s associations

00:04:14.254 --> 00:04:16.422
found the idea far too radical.

00:04:16.631 --> 00:04:18.967
But the Redstockings believed 
in the strike.

00:04:19.217 --> 00:04:23.263
So they pushed for one and slowly 
got some members on board.

00:04:23.596 --> 00:04:25.848
But overall, the committee was reluctant...

00:04:26.015 --> 00:04:27.558
until they heard...

00:04:27.558 --> 00:04:29.227
A woman around 70,

00:04:29.352 --> 00:04:32.438
she was from a 
center political party,

00:04:32.438 --> 00:04:33.940
from the old feminists.

00:04:34.065 --> 00:04:36.609
And she came to the podium and said,

00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:39.279
“Strike? Is that what you don't like?”

00:04:39.279 --> 00:04:42.156
“Why don't you then 
call it just a day off?”

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:43.700
So that's what they did.

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:47.328
The committee picked a day
when women would collectively refuse

00:04:47.328 --> 00:04:49.706
to work at home and in the office

00:04:49.706 --> 00:04:51.499
to prove their economic value.

00:04:51.833 --> 00:04:55.128
And they called it a day off 
or “kvennafrí”. 

00:04:55.586 --> 00:04:57.964
They only had three months to prepare.

00:04:58.172 --> 00:05:00.508
So the Redstockings and 
the rest of the committee

00:05:00.508 --> 00:05:04.512
immediately mobilized multiple 
labor unions and women's organizations

00:05:04.512 --> 00:05:09.100
the country to plan, fundraise, and 
spread the word about kvennafrí.

00:05:09.642 --> 00:05:12.770
The political parties used their own platform 

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:14.689
to talk to their women. 

00:05:14.689 --> 00:05:18.109
There were all kinds of women from diverse...

00:05:18.276 --> 00:05:21.321
political and social groups.

00:05:21.321 --> 00:05:24.615
And we worked very well together,
all of us.

00:05:25.033 --> 00:05:28.411
They wrote hundreds of letters 
to women across the country

00:05:28.411 --> 00:05:30.621
and called as many households 
as they could

00:05:30.621 --> 00:05:32.790
 in Iceland's phone book 
to spread the word.

00:05:32.999 --> 00:05:35.710
They wrote articles, 
did radio interviews...

00:05:36.252 --> 00:05:38.296
[in Icelandic] “The goal is for women from
all over the country

00:05:38.296 --> 00:05:41.674
to participate in this women’s holiday. 

00:05:41.674 --> 00:05:45.011
Designed fliers and organized meetings 
late into the night

00:05:45.011 --> 00:05:47.889
to make sure that kvennafrí
was a success.

00:05:48.348 --> 00:05:51.351
Some men were foolish enough

00:05:51.351 --> 00:05:54.187
to threaten women, 
threaten their employees...

00:05:54.687 --> 00:05:56.647
and that didn't go down well.

00:06:02.111 --> 00:06:04.947
On October 24th, 1975...

00:06:05.198 --> 00:06:07.784
90% of Icelandic women

00:06:07.784 --> 00:06:10.995
refused to work at home 
and in the office.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:13.664
An estimated 25,000 women

00:06:13.664 --> 00:06:15.792
took to the streets in Reykjavik alone...

00:06:16.125 --> 00:06:19.420
to give speeches and sing 
about women's rights.

00:06:19.796 --> 00:06:21.923
There is even a vinyl of recorded songs

00:06:21.923 --> 00:06:24.300
sung by the Redstockings 
from kvennafrí.

00:06:25.051 --> 00:06:28.679
[singing in Icelandic]
But dare I, want I, can I?

00:06:28.971 --> 00:06:31.808
Yes, I dare, can, and want.

00:06:31.808 --> 00:06:34.435
So many women refused to work that day

00:06:34.435 --> 00:06:36.729
that Iceland shut down.

00:06:37.063 --> 00:06:37.980
Without women

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:41.359
the telephone system crashed 
without switchboard operators

00:06:41.359 --> 00:06:44.237
newspapers couldn't publish 
without type setters.

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:46.614
The national airline canceled flights

00:06:46.614 --> 00:06:48.408
because there were no stewardesses.

00:06:48.783 --> 00:06:51.160
And preschools across the country 
shut down.

00:06:51.619 --> 00:06:54.205
Women's absence was felt in the home, too.

00:06:54.372 --> 00:06:57.500
Without domestic labor that 
Icelandic women carried out...

00:06:57.708 --> 00:06:59.001
there was no childcare.

00:06:59.127 --> 00:07:02.171
so men had to stay home or 
bring their children to work.

00:07:02.505 --> 00:07:07.885
It was this cooperation across 
politics and classes and...

00:07:08.177 --> 00:07:11.889
and housewives and women 
working outside the home.

00:07:12.098 --> 00:07:13.599
It was a celebration.

00:07:15.726 --> 00:07:19.063
The impact of kvennafrí was felt 
across the country.

00:07:19.188 --> 00:07:23.359
After the day off, Iceland passed its first 
Gender Equality Act

00:07:23.359 --> 00:07:25.736
and officially banned wage discrimination

00:07:25.736 --> 00:07:27.196
on the basis of gender.

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:30.616
Equal rights of women were added 
to the Constitution

00:07:30.616 --> 00:07:33.744
and women's representation 
in Parliament rose too.

00:07:34.036 --> 00:07:37.790
In 1980, Iecland elected 
its first female leader

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:41.752
 and the world's first woman president 
to be elected democratically.

00:07:42.170 --> 00:07:45.256
Something she insisted wouldn't 
have been possible

00:07:45.256 --> 00:07:46.632
without kvennafrí.

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:49.635
No country in the world 
has completely closed

00:07:49.635 --> 00:07:50.803
its gender pay gap.

00:07:50.803 --> 00:07:52.722
Iceland still has some work to do too.

00:07:53.139 --> 00:07:55.516
But the country's quick progress 
is a sign

00:07:55.516 --> 00:07:58.019
that closing the gap is possible.

00:07:58.561 --> 00:08:00.480
Demonstrations like kvennafrí

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:03.649
might not work in the same way 
in other countries...

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:08.029
but its success shows us what happens 
when a country unites...

00:08:08.321 --> 00:08:12.241
politically and socially to get closer 
to equality.

00:08:17.455 --> 00:08:20.082
Kvennafrí didn't stop in 1975.

00:08:20.124 --> 00:08:23.628
There was one organized 
in 1985, 2005

00:08:23.628 --> 00:08:26.839
2010, 2016, and 2018.

00:08:26.923 --> 00:08:29.634
The event has tackled everything 
from reproductive issues

00:08:29.634 --> 00:08:33.971
like abortion rights to the ongoing 
gender pay gap in Iceland.

00:08:34.347 --> 00:08:37.266
But one of my favorite pieces 
of history from this story

00:08:37.266 --> 00:08:38.601
is that you can actually listen

00:08:38.601 --> 00:08:41.979
to the original song sung 
on the 1975 day off

00:08:41.979 --> 00:08:43.064
off on Spotify.

00:08:43.064 --> 00:08:45.358
Which includes song sung by 
the Redstockings.

00:08:45.650 --> 00:08:47.568
The link is in the description down below.

00:08:47.944 --> 00:08:50.446
Thanks again for watching 
this episode of Missing Chapter

00:08:50.446 --> 00:08:52.031
and we can't wait to see you 
in the next one.

00:08:52.406 --> 00:08:53.074
Till next time.

